pe == mimetype:\n            return _load_lexers(entry)(**options)\n    return ClassNotFound...\n```\n\nHmm. Actually I recall there IS a prefix fallback in some versions. Let me check my memory of pygments source (2.11-ish):\n\n```python\ndef get_lexer_for_mimetype(_mimetype, **options):\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a lexer for a mimetype.\n    \"\"\"\n    for mimetype, entry in _mimetype_lextab:\n        if _mimetype == mimetype:\n            return _load_lexers(entry)(**options)\n\n    # Fallback to `text/x-...` prefixes\n    for mimetype, entry in _mimetype_lextab:\n        if _mimetype.startswith(mimetype):\n            return _load_lexers(entry)(**options)\n\n    raise ClassNotFound('no lexer for mimetype %r found' % _mimetype)\n```\n\nHmm, I genuinely don't remember a startswith fallback. Let me think about httpie's actual tests. httpie has tests for this in `tests/test_cli.py` or `tests/test_outputs.py`? Actually there's `tests/test_utils.py`? Let me recall httpie's repo: `httpie/output/formatters/colors.py` contains `get_lexer`. Tests in `tests/test_output_colors`? Hmm, I don't remember exactly.\n\nActually, I recall httpie's colors.py:\n\n```python\ndef get_lexer(mime, explicit_json=False, body=''):\n    # Build candidate mime type and lexer names.\n    mime_types, lexer_names = [mime], []\n    type_, subtype = mime.split('/', 1)\n    if '+' not in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append(subtype)\n    else:\n        subtype_name, subtype_suffix = subtype.split('+', 1)\n        lexer_names.extend([subtype_name, subtype_suffix])\n        mime_types.extend([\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_name),\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_suffix)\n        ])\n\n    # As a last resort, if no lexer feels responsible, and\n    # the subtype contains 'json', take the JSON lexer\n    if 'json' in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append('json')\n\n    # Try to resolve the right lexer.\n    lexer = None\n    for mime_type in mime_types:\n        try:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype(mime_type)\n            break\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            pass\n    else:\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n\n    if explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)):\n        # JSON response with an incorrect Content-Type?\n        try:\n            json.loads(body)  # FIXME: the body also gets parsed in json.py\n        except ValueError:\n            pass  # Nope\n        else:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')\n\n    return lexer\n```\n\nYes, that matches.\n\nNow, real-world behavior: For 'text/plain', get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/plain') returns TextLexer. For 'application/json', JsonLexer.\n\nFor the explicit_json branch:\n- Case A: explicit_json=True, body valid JSON, mime 'text/plain' \u2192 lexer is TextLexer \u2192 json.loads succeeds \u2192 lexer becomes JsonLexer. Covers 147-154 (all of it: condition true, try, else).\n- Case B: explicit_json=True, body invalid JSON ('{'), mime 'text/plain' \u2192 TextLexer, json.loads raises ValueError \u2192 pass \u2192 lexer stays TextLexer. Covers 151-152.\n- Case C: explicit_json=True, body valid JSON, mime 'application/json' \u2192 lexer is JsonLexer (not TextLexer) \u2192 condition false \u2192 skip. Covers the false branch of 147.\n- Case D: explicit_json=True, body valid JSON, mime 'foo/bar' \u2192 lexer None \u2192 condition true (not lexer) \u2192 json.loads succeeds \u2192 lexer = JsonLexer. Covers `not lexer` branch.\n- Case E: explicit_json=False \u2192 condition false regardless.\n- Case F: explicit_json=True, body='' \u2192 condition false (body falsy).\n\nNow the tricky branch: lines 141-145 (the else clause of the mime loop). To hit it, all mime_types must fail. Real cases:\n\n- 'foo/bar': mime_types=['foo/bar'], both lookups fail \u2192 lexer None. Covers 141-14